<h2>A marriage litigated for over a decade</h2>
<p>The parties married on 5 May 2009 at Champa, Chhattisgarh. The wife left for her parental home within months, on 23 July 2009, and each side has blamed the other ever since. The husband said she demanded accommodation separate from his parents; the wife said he left her at her parental home to cool a dispute between his sister and her brother, and then simply never took her back. A maintenance case under Section 125 CrPC was dismissed in January 2017, and the revision against it failed in 2018.</p>
<p>Two suits followed in 2019 before the Family Court at Jamshedpur: the husband&rsquo;s divorce suit on cruelty and desertion (Original Suit No. 584 of 2019), and the wife&rsquo;s petition for restitution of conjugal rights under Section 9 (Original Suit No. 313 of 2019). The Family Court dismissed the divorce suit and decreed restitution in the wife&rsquo;s favour, directing the husband to restore conjugal life. The husband filed first appeals against both decrees under Section 19(1) of the Family Courts Act, 1984, which were heard together; First Appeal No. 177 of 2024, against the dismissal of his divorce suit, is the subject of this note.</p>

<h2>What the Division Bench held</h2>
<p>Dismissing the divorce appeal, the Bench found that the Family Court had consciously evaluated the evidence and that its judgment and decree did not fall within the ambit of perversity. Two findings carried the day.</p>

<table class="law">
<tr><th>Ground pleaded</th><th>What the courts found</th></tr>
<tr><td>Cruelty — Section 13(1)(ia)</td><td>Mere arguments or a desire for separate accommodation do not amount to cruelty. No grave and weighty conduct was proved against the wife.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Desertion — Section 13(1)(ib)</td><td>The record showed it was the husband who was not willing to keep his wife. A separation sustained by the petitioner&rsquo;s own unwillingness cannot found a desertion plea.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The appellate standard, restated</h2>
<p>The judgment is useful chiefly for its restatement of the appellate court&rsquo;s role. A first appeal under Section 19 of the Family Courts Act is a full appeal on facts and law, and the High Court can reappraise the evidence. But reappraisal is not substitution. As the Bench put it, perversity is a high threshold for intervention, distinguishing it from a mere error of fact or law. Where the trial court&rsquo;s view is a possible view, supported by the record and reached after a conscious evaluation of the evidence, the decree stands even if another bench might have weighed matters differently.</p>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>What appellants must show to cross the perversity bar:</strong></p>
<p>A finding resting on no evidence at all; material evidence ignored or misread in a manner that changes the outcome; a conclusion no reasonable court could reach on the record; or a misapplication of the statutory ground itself. Dissatisfaction with how the trial court balanced witnesses is not enough.</p>
</div>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<p>For husbands and wives alike, the case underlines that matrimonial appeals are won at trial. The evidentiary record closes before the Family Court; an appeal argued on the same thin record will meet the same result. Where the real grievance is the breakdown of the marriage rather than any provable matrimonial fault, parties are often better served exploring mediation and a mutual consent divorce under Section 13B than litigating fault grounds through two tiers of courts.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>The companion appeal, against the restitution decree in the wife&rsquo;s favour, was heard along with this appeal. The principles governing restitution decrees — and their limits — form a distinct body of law, and a decree under Section 9 has its own consequences, including under Section 13(1A) where cohabitation is not resumed for a year after the decree.</p>
</div>
